{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788377340,"nonce":"PCVRcxSfLuBWQMZQ/jbhijgc4Xg=","title":"designate hunt: no verb publishes an animal's manhunter chance, and composition reports the TERRAIN under the animal instead of the animal","message":"Found in play, run openrun-20260902 day 6 (summer 1). Dorian caught the bad\ncall; the mod had the information and put it where the decision could not use it.\n\n## What happened\n\nFive hunts designated in one call — 2 emu, 1 ostrich, 1 iguana, 1 monitor\nlizard — against a roster whose ONLY shooter is Shooting 3 with the\nTrigger-happy trait. Emu and Ostrich both carry\n`race.manhunterOnDamageChance = 1.00` and both MOVE FASTER THAN A COLONIST\n(6.0 and 5.5 vs a human's ~4.6), so a botched shot is an unbreakable melee\nfight with the colony's only rifle. That is a colonist-losing call and the\nenvelope gave no sign of it.\n\n## Where the information actually is\n\n**1. 's  describes the GROUND, not the target.** For a\nhunt batch it came back:\n\n    composition: [{def:'Sand', by:'terrain', count:3},\n                  {def:'Gravel', by:'terrain', count:1},\n                  {def:'Sandstone_Rough', by:'terrain', count:1}]\n\nFive animals designated and not one animal named. `composition` is documented\nin `checklists/turn.md` as the answer `accepted: N` never was — 'the per-def\nrollup of what actually landed'. For `mine-vein` it is exactly that\n(`MineableSteel: 4, Marble: 8`). For `hunt` the per-def rollup of what landed\nshould be `Emu: 2, Ostrich: 1, Iguana: 1, MonitorLizard: 1`, and instead the\nrollup keys on the terrain the pawn happens to be standing on.\n\n**2. The warning exists but arrives AFTER the decision, on a different\nchannel.** The game's own caution fired into the journal at designate time:\n\n    seq 285  'Warning: Emus have a 100% base chance to attack when harmed.\n              Chance depends on range and hunter stealth stat.'\n    seq 286  'Warning: Ostriches have a 100% base chance to attack when harmed.'\n\nThose are journal `message` rows, not fields on the result. A caller reading\nthe envelope — which is what the protocol asks for — sees `accepted: 5,\ndesignated: 5, unreachable: 0` and nothing else. The information only reaches\nyou on the NEXT `journal` read, by which point the designation is placed.\n\n**3. No read verb carries it either.** Checked: `pawns` rows are\n`{id,name,class,kind,faction,at,mood_pct,health_pct,job,attention_rank}`;\n`things --def Emu --detail` gives a stack rollup. Neither publishes\n`manhunterOnDamageChance`, `manhunterOnTameFailChance`, `baseBodySize` or\n`MoveSpeed`. So there is no way to answer 'is this animal safe to hunt' from\nthe observation surface at all — the numbers above were read out of\n`Data/Core/Defs` XML on the host, which an unattended agent has no business\ndoing mid-run.\n\n## Why it matters beyond hunting\n\n`manhunterOnTameFailChance` is the same shape for standing rule 9 ('Tame\nanimals'): a failed tame on an Ostrich is a 10% manhunter roll, on an Emu it\nis **100%**. The run contract tells the agent to tame, and the surface cannot\ntell it which tames bite back.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `designate {type:'hunt'|'tame'|'slaughter'}`'s `composition` rolls up by the\n  TARGET's def, not the terrain under it — `Emu: 2, Ostrich: 1` — matching what\n  `mine-vein` already does for rock.\n- Each such row carries the retaliation numbers the decision needs:\n  `manhunter_on_damage`, `manhunter_on_tame_fail`, `body_size`, `move_speed`,\n  read off `RaceProps`/`statBases`. A row with `manhunter_on_damage \u003e= 1.0`\n  is the one a caller must be able to see WITHOUT a second call.\n- A hunt/tame batch containing any 100%-retaliation target reports it on the\n  RESULT — as a `caution` block beside `composition`, not only as a journal\n  message. It need not refuse: hunting an emu with four good shooters is\n  correct play. It must not be silent.\n- `pawns` (or a `pawn` section) publishes the same fields for wildlife, so\n  'what is safe to hunt on this map' is answerable before any designation is\n  sent.\n- Cross-check: the terrain rollup is not wrong for every type — for `mine`,\n  `smooth-floor` and `remove-floor` the terrain IS the target. The fix is to\n  key `composition` on what the designation actually acts on, per type, rather\n  than to replace it globally.","files":null}]}